dos_and_donts:
Do(s): 
Have a good friend circle; 
practice cases whenever possible; 
Practice good cases as much as possible (quality is sometimes better than quantity);
Retrospect (most of the times I have failed to solve correctly during practice sessions with my friends or consulting sessions organized by consulting club so I used to reanalyze the cases, understand what I missed; if the case was easy and small, how can we make it more complicated, what elements can be introduced; sort of made a notebook to pen down all of them; further try to create your own notes of framework with all the elements);
understand underlying structure for guesstimates (explained in IIMA casebook and analyze solutions given by consulting club for the weekly guesstimate series);
practice and read the basic data required for guesstimate
Don't(s):Do not....
Second guess your preparation level, utilize that time to take rest, apply, fill forms, give exams and prepare even more;
Doubt yourself because of rejections, sometimes the roles and the company expectations are different;

interview_outline:
Interview 1:Balanced 2 panelists, 1 technical and 1 HR
Run me through your resume; Questions on workex;
Case: Unconventional: Your client is facing continuous complaints from its customers regarding the water purifier which they manufacture. The company is operative since the past 10 years and the issue is being faced since the last 6 months. You have been hired to find the reason behind the complaints and solve the same. 
 Why consulting; What if there is no consensus in the team, how would you tackle the situation;
Interview 2: Technical heavy, importance of data was focused upon, 1 panelist technical
Tell me about yourself; how is the weather and stuffs like that, friendly conversation;
Case:There is a biweekly meeting going on with the CEO of the company. The sales have been very low. The sales and marketing team says that they did not receive the product and thus they couldn't give it to the distributors resulting in declined sales. Meanwhile, the operations team claims that they have a huge finished good inventory with them and there has been no issues with the production of the parts. The CEO is angry and is frustrated. You enter the meeting room. how will you change this scenario and help the CEO out in solving this?
Interviewer was sometimes trying to grill, but answering them the answers truthfully on why you think as such with good reasoning will surely help.
Converted.

pre_process_tips:
Round 1: Online aptitude test followed by automated video interview in the same slot; 
Round 2: Buddy Call (15 minutes)
Round 3 and following: Multiple Offline Interviews

interview_outline:
Introduction, Asked about summer internship in detail; 
Why this sector, Why Hero?, Long term plans; 
What do you understand from Supply chain management, How will you decide the costing of different parts supplied;
How will you get things done from people way above your age(any instance from the past); Are you good in negotiating, give instances; 
Interests and hobbies(in detail)
